\par }\pard \ltrpar\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs20\insrsid4402984 There are 4 input/output verbs : OPEN, READ, WRITE, CLOSE.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s9\qj \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\outlinelevel8\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs28\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs28\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid4402984 OPEN statement
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s59\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs20\insrsid4402984 Before an input or an output file can be used by the program it must be opened. An OPEN statement, designates files as either input or output. It also accesses the specific de
vices, and makes the files available for processing. It performs header label routines if label records are STANDARD. The OPEN statement checks the header label to determine if the correct file has been accessed. 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\qj \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs20\insrsid4402984 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs20\ul\insrsid4402984 Eg 8.4:
\par }\pard \ltrpar\qj \li0\ri0\widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp80 \brdrb\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20 \brdrr\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp80 \wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f1\fs20\insrsid4402984 OPEN INPUT EMPLOYEE-FILE.
\par OPEN OUTPUT REPORT-FILE.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\qj \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs20\insrsid4402984 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s59\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs20\insrsid4402984 
The order in which files are opened is not significant. The only restriction is that a file must be opened before it may be read or written; a file must be accessed before it may be processed. Since the OPEN statement accesses the files,
 it is generally on of the first instructions coded in the PROCEDURE DIVISION.
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\qj \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs20\insrsid4402984 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s9\qj \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\outlinelevel8\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs28\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs28\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid4402984 READ statement
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs20\insrsid4402984 After an input file has been opened, it may be read. A READ statement transmits data from the input device, assigned in the ENVIRONMENT DIVISION, to the input storage area, defined in the FILE SECTION of the DATA DIVISION. 
\par The primary function of the READ statement is to transmit one data record to the input area reserved for that file. That is, each time a READ statement is executed, one record is read into primary storage.
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s34\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs20\insrsid4402984 
The READ statement has, however, several other functions. Like the OPEN statement, it performs certain checks. It checks the length of each input record to ensure that it corresponds to the length specified in a RECORD CONTAINS clause in the d
ata DIVISION. If a discrepancy exists, an error message prints, and a program interrupt occurs.
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs20\insrsid4402984 The READ statement will also use the BLOCK CONTAINS clause, if specified, to perform a check on the blocking factor.
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s59\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs20\insrsid4402984 The AT END clause in the READ statement tests to determine if there is any more input. An AT END clause of the READ statement tells the computer what to do if there is no more data to be read.
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\qj \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
