\fs20\insrsid4402984 Records in files frequently must be sorted into specific sequences for updating, answering inquiries, or generating reports. Sorting is a common procedure used for arranging records into a specific order so 
that sequential processing can be performed.
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\qj \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs20\insrsid4402984 \tab COBOL has a SORT verb, which can make it very useful as part of a COBOL program. 
\par \tab The programmer must specify whether the key field is to be an ASCENDING KEY or a DESCENDING KEY, depending on which sequence is required :
\par \tab ASCENDING : From lowest to highest
\par \tab DESCENDING : From highest to lowest
\par \tab The SORT verb may be used to sequence records with more than one key field. For example, to sort an employee file so that it is in alphabetic sequence by name within each department.
\par 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs20\ul\insrsid4402984 Eg 9.1:
