The first section in the PROCEDURE DIVISION contains the SORT instruction, any processing to be performed before or after the SORT verb is executed, and a STOP RUN.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\langfenp1033\insrsid4402984 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}
The second section begins with the main module of the INPUT PROCEDURE. It opens the input file, reads the first record, and then performs a process routine (in a separate paragraph within this second section) until there is no more data.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\langfenp1033\insrsid4402984 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}After the separate paragraph is executed until ARE-THER-MORE-RECORDS = \'93NO\'94
, control returns to the main module of the second section to be terminated, control must pass to the last statement within the section. This means that a GO TO is required. We code GO TO A300-TES
T-IT-EXIT as the last sentence. Since no operations are required in this last paragraph, EXIT is coded, which passes control back to the SORT statement, where the file is then sorted.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\qj \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs20\insrsid4402984 
