\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid4402984 Using ALTERNATE RECORD KEYs
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s57\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs20\insrsid4402984 Indexed files may be created with, and accessed by, more than one identifying key field. That is, we
 may want to access employee records using the name as the key field. To enable a file to be accessed randomly using more than one key field, we would need to establish an ALTERNATE RECORD KEY.
\par To establish multiple key fields for indexing, we use an ALTERNATE RECORD KEY clause in the SELECT statement.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\s57\qj \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs20\insrsid4402984 
