\par }\pard \ltrpar\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f1\fs20\insrsid4402984 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s9\qj \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\outlinelevel8\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs28\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs28\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid4402984 Accessing records randomly by alternate record key
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s34\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs20\insrsid4402984 
The program that accesses the file by key field has the same SELECT clause except that ACCESS IS RANDOM rather than SEQUENTIAL. In the PROCEDURE DIVISION , we can access records by either I-EMP-NO , the record key, or I-EMP-DEPT, the alternate key.
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs20\insrsid4402984 The KEY
 clause is used with the READ statement when an indexed file has ALTERNATE RECORD KEYs that we want to use to randomly access a record. If the KEY clause is omitted when accessing a file randomly, the RECORD KEY is assumed to be the KEY used for finding t
he record.
\par Suppose ALTERNATE RECORD KEY WITH DUPLICATES was specified in the ENVIRONMENT DIVISION and there is more than one record with the same ALTERNATE RECORD KEY. The first one that was actually placed on the disk will be the one retrieved by the READ.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s9\qj \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\outlinelevel8\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs28\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs28\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid4402984 The START statement
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs20\insrsid4402984 The START statement enables a program to begin processing an indexed file sequentially but at a record location other than the first or next physical record in the file. The access of the file is to be in sequence (ACCESS IS SEQUENTIA
L) if we use the RECORD KEY for finding a record, even though we want to start the access at some point other than the beginning. The ACCESS IS DYNAMIC clause is used if we want to begin the processing an indexed file based on the contents of the ALTERNAT
E RECORD KEY. 
\par When the record to be accessed has a key equal to the one placed in the RECORD KEY, the KEY clause in the START statement is not required. The INVALID clause is executed only if no such record is found.
\par Note that the START locates the desired record but it does not READ it into storage. The record must always  be brought into storage with a READ statement. 
\par }\pard \ltrpar\qj \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs20\insrsid4402984 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs20\ul\insrsid4402984 Eg 12.6:
\par }\pard \ltrpar\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp80 \brdrb\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp20 \brdrr\brdrs\brdrw45\brsp80 \wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af1\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f1\fs20\insrsid4402984 MOVE \'93Y\'94 TO WS-FOUND.
\par MOVE 1001 TO I-EMP-NO.
\par START IND-EMP-FILE
\par \tab KEY > I-EMP-NO
\par \tab INVALID KEY DISPLAY \'93THERE IS NO EMP NO > 1001\'94
\par \tab \tab              MOVE \'93N\'94 TO WS-FOUND.
\par IF WS-FOUND = \'93Y\'94
\par       READ IND-EMP-FILE
\par \tab AT END
\par \tab   MOVE \'93Y\'94 TO WS-EOF.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs20\insrsid4402984 
\par Suppose we wish to begin pro
cessing with an I-EMP-NO greater than 006. We must include a KEY clause with the START because we wish to position the file at allocation greater than the value of a RECORD KEY. The KEY clause can be omitted only if the record to be located has a RECORD K
EY equal to the one stored.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s9\qj \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\outlinelevel8\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs28\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs28\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid4402984 The ACCESS IS DYNAMIC clause
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs20\insrsid4402984 Sometimes we wish to access an indexed file both randomly and sequentially in a single program. For this, we say that ACCESS IS DYNAMIC. 
\par In addition to using ACCESS IS DYNAMIC for combining sequenti
al and random access techniques in a single program, we can use this clause to access records by ALTERNATE RECORD KEY. Also, when records ate to be accessed by both RECORD KEY and ALTERNATE RECORD KEY, use ACCESS IS DYNAMIC.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s9\qj \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\outlinelevel8\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs28\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs28\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid4402984 Rules for using the START statement
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\langfenp1033\insrsid4402984 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard\plain \ltrpar\qj \fi-360\li720\ri0\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\wrapdefault\faauto\ls20\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid4728883 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs20\insrsid4402984 
The file must be accessed with (a) ACCESS IS SEQUENTIAL for reading records in sequence by the RECORD KEY or (b) ACCESS IS DYNAMIC for reading records in sequence by an ALTERNATE RECORD KEY.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\langfenp1033\insrsid4402984 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}The file must be opened as either input or I-O.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\langfenp1033\insrsid4402984 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}If the KEY phrase is omitted, the relational operator \'93IS EQUAL TO\'94
 is implied and the primary record key is assumed to be the key of reference.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\langnp1033\langfenp1033\insrsid4402984 \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 4.\tab}
We use KEY =, >, NOT < for accessing records by ALTERNATE RECORD KEY. We also use KEY >, NOT < for accessing records by a value that is correspondingly >, NOT < the primary RECORD KEY.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs20\insrsid4402984 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s9\qj \li0\ri0\keepn\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\outlinelevel8\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs28\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\fs28\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid4402984 The READ\'85NEXT RECORD \'85 Instruction
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\qj \fi720\li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid16136647 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs20\insrsid4402984 To process records both randomly and sequentially in a single file, ACCESS IS DYNAMIC  must be specified. To indicate that we wish to read record
s in sequence by some key field from a file accessed dynamically, we must use a NEXT RECORD clause. The READ\'85
NEXT RECORD is also used for sequentially reading from a file by this ALTERNATE RECORD KEY, or, for beginning a sequential read at some point other than the beginning of a file.
